history.idea:
Replace the raw shuffle with a filter for clean, intact, in-domain (WikiText-style) documents, de-duplicated, in seeded random order.

1. Dedup: keep the first occurrence of each doc, keyed by whitespace-normalised lowercase text, and also by its first 300 characters to catch near-duplicates.

2. Word-order / domain score: lowercase and whitespace-split. Build the set B of all word bigrams in target_sample. For each pool doc, score s = fraction of the bigrams in its first 1500 words that are in B. Shuffled-word articles, spam and web text should score far below intact wiki text.

3. Threshold calibration without labels: build bigrams from 100 of the target docs, score the other 100, then swap the halves. Take tau = 0.8 x the 5th percentile of those held-out scores. Since B from all 200 docs is larger, real scores sit slightly higher, so this is conservative. Keep docs with s >= tau.

4. Cheap junk guards. Drop a doc if any of these hold:
   - fewer than 200 characters;
   - it does not start with '= ' (target articles all start with a '= Title =' header);
   - more than 5% of its characters are non-ASCII letters (homoglyph damage);
   - it matches spam patterns ('discount code', 'coupon', 'free shipping', 'best price guaranteed');
   - its most frequent 5-word shingle makes up more than 5% of its shingles (repetition junk).

5. Output: the kept indices, shuffled with random.Random(CURATE_SEED). If they total fewer than T tokens (estimate tokens as len(text)/4, or use tiktoken if time allows), let the trainer cycle the list rather than padding with rejected docs.

Print the kept count and kept token estimate for diagnosis.
